To create a log aggregation system, we'll need to set up an ELK stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana), as it's one of the most widely used open-source log management solutions.

**1. Setup Elasticsearch**

Firstly, install and run Elasticsearch in your desired environment (cloud, on-premises). We won't be writing code to interact with Elasticsearch directly since we'll be using Logstash and Kibana for parsing and indexing respectively.

```
# If you are setting up Elasticsearch on a local machine or server
wget https://artifacts.elastic.co/downloads/elasticsearch/elasticsearch-7.10.1-amd64.deb
sudo dpkg -i elasticsearch-7.10.1-amd64.deb
sudo /etc/init.d/elasticsearch start
```

**2. Setup Logstash**

Logstash is a server-side data processing pipeline that ingests data from multiple sources, transforms it, and sends to a "stash" like Elasticsearch.

To create a simple Logstash pipeline in JSON configuration file:

```json
input {
  file {
    path => "/path/to/your/log/file.log"
    start_position => "beginning"
    sincedb_path => "/dev/null"
    type => "accesslog"
  }
}

filter {
  grok {
    match => { "message" => "%{SYSLOG5424SD:systemd_sd} %{GREEDYDATA:message}" }
  }

  date {
    match => [ "timestamp", "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%NZ"]
  }
}

output {
  elasticsearch {
    hosts => ["http://localhost:9200"] 
    index => "syslog-%{+YYYY.MM.dd}"
  }
}
```

In this configuration file, we're receiving logs from a log file, parsing it using the Grok filter plugin (which implements a pattern recognition algorithm to identify different parts of each log line), and sending them into Elasticsearch for storage and search.

**3. Setup Kibana**

Kibana lets you visualize your data in various charts and graphs, providing insights through interactive dashboards.

Kibana is bundled with the Elasticsearch package, so if you've installed Elasticsearch as described above, Kibana should be available at http://localhost:5601.

**4. Test the system**

With everything set up, it's time to test our log aggregation system. Place some logs in the file that Logstash is reading from, and then check Kibana. You should start seeing the data visualized automatically within a few minutes.

This is a very basic setup of an ELK stack for log aggregation. Depending on your needs, you may need to set up more complex configurations or add additional features like data shipping with Beats or security with Shield. For example, if you want to aggregate logs from multiple sources other than files (like network devices or cloud services), consider using Elasticsearch's sister project Heartbeat for network device monitoring and Filebeat for lightweight local file harvesting.

This solution will only work locally on your machine or server, if you want to use it in a production environment, you might need to set up an ELK stack with cloud-based solutions. AWS offers the Elastic Beanstalk service which can deploy and run the ELK stack automatically.

**5. Advanced Configuration**

You may want more advanced configurations like setting up Logstash as a system D service, setting up SSL/TLS for Elasticsearch communication, or fine-tuning your data retention policy in Elasticsearch.

This solution is not meant to be exhaustive but is a good start to get you familiar with ELK stack log aggregation system. You can find extensive documentation on the official ELK website and other online resources.

Remember that handling logs involves sensitive information (user data, errors, debugging information), so make sure your stack is configured securely according to industry best practices!

