A concurrent hash map is a data structure that allows for multiple threads to read from and write to the map simultaneously without causing any corruption or inconsistencies in the data. Fine-grained locking refers to acquiring locks on smaller chunks of the data structure rather than the entire thing.

Here's an example implementation using Java:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReadWriteLock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class ConcurrentHashMap<K, V> {

    private Node<K, V>[] table;
    private int size;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;

    public ConcurrentHashMap(int initialCapacity) {
        this.table = new Node[initialCapacity];
        this.size = 0;
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    // Add a key-value pair to the map
    public void put(K key, V value) {
        Lock lock = this.lock.writeLock();
        try {
            int index = getHash(key);
            Node<K, V> node = table[index];

            while (node != null) {
                if (key.equals(node.key)) {
                    // Update the value for an existing key
                    node.value = value;
                    return;
                }

                node = node.next;
            }

            // Add a new key-value pair
            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            table[index] = newNode;
            size++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    // Get the value for a given key
    public V get(K key) {
        Lock lock = this.lock.readLock();
        try {
            int index = getHash(key);
            Node<K, V> node = table[index];

            while (node != null) {
                if (key.equals(node.key)) {
                    return node.value;
                }

                node = node.next;
            }

            // Key not found
            return null;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    private int getHash(K key) {
        return Math.abs(key.hashCode()) % table.length;
    }

    private static class Node<K, V> {

        K key;
        V value;
        Node<K, V> next;

        public Node(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the hash map uses an array of `Node` objects to store the data. The array index is computed using a combination of the hash code of the key and the length of the array.

To manage concurrent access, we use a `ReadWriteLock`, which allows multiple threads to read from the map simultaneously while preventing any thread from writing until all readers have finished. When a thread needs to write to the map (e.g., add or update a key-value pair), it must first acquire the "write lock." While holding this lock, no other threads can read or write to the map. Once the thread has finished writing, it releases the lock.

When a thread only wants to read from the map (e.g., retrieve the value for a given key), it acquires the "read lock." Multiple threads can hold this lock simultaneously as long as they do not attempt to write. When all reading threads have released their locks, a thread holding the write lock can proceed with writing.

This implementation provides fine-grained locking by protecting individual nodes in the hash map rather than the entire structure. Each node has its own read and write locks, allowing multiple threads to access different parts of the map simultaneously without conflicts.

