Linux capabilities are a feature introduced in the 2.6.26 kernel that allows system call interfaces to be made more secure by limiting their potential impact.

The Linux kernel has a permission system called "capabilities" which is designed to limit the power of individual processes, especially those started by set-user-id (SUID) executables, to prevent them from making certain types of harmful system calls. This provides an additional level of security beyond traditional file permissions and Access Control Lists (ACLs).

Each process in Linux has a capabilities set associated with it, which is defined as a bitwise mask that indicates the allowed capabilities. There are 19 different capability types ranging from CAP_AUDIT_ADMIN to CAP_SYSLOG.

Here's a brief overview of some commonly used capabilities:

- CAP_CHOWN: Allows changing ownership of files.
- CAP_DAC_READ_WRITE: Allows access to /dev/caps, allowing a process to be granted additional capabilities by the kernel.
- CAP_FOWNER: Allows most of the file capabilities to be set, similar to CAP_CHOWN but for other users' files that the process owns.
- CAP_SYSLOG: Allows writing log messages with the log authority.
- CAP_SYS_NICE: Allows raising and lowering process priorities.
- CAP_SYS_RESOURCE: Allows controlling various system resources such as quotas and timesharing classes.
- CAP_SYS_TIME: Allows setting the system clock.

Creating an auditing tool:

Below is a simple Perl script that reads the /proc filesystem to identify which capabilities are set for each process. This can be useful to check if any processes have more capabilities than necessary, increasing potential security risks.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;

# Get list of all PIDs from /proc directory
my @pids;
foreach my $dir (glob "/proc/*") {
    next unless $dir =~ /^(\d+)/; # Only interested in subdirectories with PID numbers.
    push(@pids, $1);
}

foreach my $pid (@pids) {
    print "Checking process $pid...\n";

    # Map the capability strings to their numerical values
    my %capability_to_num = map { $_ => $capnum++ } grep /^CAP_/ =>
        keys %{&capabilities};

    # Get the capabilities of this PID.
    my @capabilities;
    open(my $fh, "<", "/proc/$pid/capabilities")
        or die "Could not read /proc/$pid/capabilities: $!";
    while (<$fh>) {
        chomp;
        my ($cap, $val) = split(/\s+/, $_);
        push(@capabilities, "$cap $capability_to_num{$cap}");
    }
    close($fh);

    # Print the capabilities
    print "  - @capabilities\n";
}

# The capabilities subroutine is defined here as an example.
sub capabilities {
    return {
        'CAP_CHOWN' => 1,
        'CAP_DAC_READ_WRITE' => 2,
        ...
        # Add all other capabilities with their values.
    };
}
```

This script will read the /proc/<PID>/capabilities file for each running process and print the capabilities. It also includes a `capabilities` subroutine to map capability strings to their numerical values, which are used in /proc/<PID>/capabilities.

